      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "An IgE-mediated allergic reaction to an antihistamine sounds like a paradox and is rare."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To describe 2 patients with anaphylaxis caused by mizolastine. In one patient, differential diagnosis from food allergy was necessary, whereas in the other patient, the history was clear."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "History and in vivo skin testing were used for diagnosis. A challenge test to mizolastine was also proposed, but both patients refused to give consent."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Skin test results were positive to mizolastine, whereas tests to the inert ingredients of the mizolastine tablets and to other H1 and H2 blockers had negative results."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "In vivo tests are highly sensitive, and they confirmed the diagnosis of the uncommon antihistamine allergy."
        }
      ]},
